Back Forty Bullseye

Farmer in Iowa starts his day.  Cattle to feed.  Fences to mend.  In great detail, describe the land he purchased from his grandfather and his appreciation for nature, honest hard work, clean air, etc.  Listening to bob-white quail, locusts, etc.  Fence repaired, headed to the spring for lunch and crystal clear, ice cold spring water.  Listening to the futures reports on the radio as he basks in the sweet morning sunshine.  St Louis pork bellies up 5 and a quarter…   November wheat up 6 and a half…  (hears the twang of a bowstring) looking like a prosperous year at this rate.   
He looks up into the sky, wiping the morning’s sweat from the back of his neck. Squinting, he spots something odd.  Before he can react, the arrow slices into his eye. The tri-pointed head silences his thoughts…  instantly… permanently.
As his body slumps to the ground, only the AM radio bears witness to his demise: “... and that plucky young man stuck to his resolution. Later becoming the spouse of our first openly gay President.  You probably know him by his married name (dramatic pause) Eleanor Roosevelt.  And now you know, the rest of the story.  Paul Harvey…  good day!” 

    + [Terminate Simulation] ->->
